Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: The Brake Shoes Bleed Copper
- Story & Context: The genre’s opening shot exploded during a zero-degree run outside the San Ysidro Rail Depot when a frozen brake pad melted against the locomotive wheel, filling coach car seven with an acrid cloud of burning copper dust. Instead of pulling the emergency cord, Héctor Cruz jammed his baritone sax straight into the car’s broken PA speaker while six factory workers synced their pocket synths to the train's frantic, uneven deceleration. Sister Marisol gripped the luggage rail, watching sparks shear across the trackbed, and delivered the track's seminal rallying cry: "Hold the third rail until your marrow rattles." The resulting sound became the first recognized Tumbatren recording, preserved directly onto an unspooled micro-cassette recorder tucked inside an abandoned metal lunchbox.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Cinematic macro shot of rusted train brake shoes screaming against polished steel rails, erupting in bright copper sparks and violet sulfur smoke; cold desert gravel below and an ominous, fog-drowned train station in the blurred distance; cinematic dark art, deep blacks and fiery amber illumination.

Track 2: Who Cleared the Track for Sister Marisol?
- Story & Context: Recorded during an unmapped delay on the sidings of Empalme Junction, this confession piece documents the night Sister Marisol deliberately keyed fake signals into the trackside dispatcher box so the night train would bypass her home village. Shivering in her damp poncho, smelling of diesel exhaust and stale instant coffee, she used her sequencer to loop the rhythmic clicking of the rail relays beneath Cruz’s weeping, sub-octave sax lines. When asked by the bewildered conductor why she refused to let the doors open, she leaned into her clipped lapel mic and whispered: "I skipped my station to feed this battery to the dead." The track captures the exact moment the exhausted passengers ceased demanding to go home and instead began dancing in the darkened aisle.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Atmospheric noir visual of an abandoned railway platform at 3:00 AM under a rain-streaked sodium vapor lamp; an empty timetable board swings on chains, its yellowed paper peeling away to reveal scratched electrical schematics; spectral condensation pooling on rusted iron benches, hyper-detailed, melancholic.

Track 3: El Chicle del Último Riel
- Story & Context: According to railway lore, the final track was salvaged from an uncoupled caboose found stranded on the dried salt flats of Sonora with its battery instruments still humming at 102 BPM across salt-crusted linoleum. Station agents discovered only Cruz’s abandoned baritone sax resting on an oil drum, its bell caked in fine white desert dust and tied with frayed conductor wire. Local trackmen still claim that on moonless nights, the spectral rhythm of the lost train vibrates up through the iron tracks, carrying Marisol's disembodied toast over heavy sousaphone drops: "The rails remember what the dispatch erased." The recording stands as the genre's mythic epitaph, where the clatter of the machine finally swallowed its players whole.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Haunting wide shot of twin railway tracks stretching toward an infinite salt flat under a starless, ink-black sky; a single antique baritone saxophone lies horizontally across the rails, coated in crystalline salt, with green and red switch-lantern lights glowing like phantom eyes far down the vanishing point.

